\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{=}7$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$, so a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper content.
Even frontier LLMs exhibit comparable collapse beyond $\Kempirical$: per-depth recall at $d \geq 2$ ranges 8--38\% (vs.\ 20\% chance), indicating error redistribution rather than genuine depth recovery (Appendix).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$) followed by rapid convergence, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$).
The retrain-chain ($\lambda = 0.285$, no cliff) reflects cumulative model-adaptation artifacts, extending $\Kempirical$ to 7 (Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).

\paragraph{Domain and representation dependence.}
C4 yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (8/9 configurations), Python code $\Kempirical{=}2$ for four models ($\Kempirical{=}1$ for Pythia-1.4B; Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}), arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (five models, four families), and Wikipedia $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}); the ordering correlates with structural complexity (Table~\ref{tab:domain-ttr-entropy}).
The only C4 exception (Mistral-7B, $\Kempirical{=}1$) yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ on code and $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv, confirming domain structure as the primary factor (Appendix~\ref{sec:latex_ablation}).
$\Kempirical$ is also representation-dependent ($K^*_{\text{neural}} = 3$ for Qwen vs.\ $2$ for Pythia/OLMo) and scorer-dependent (cross-family scoring preserves $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 C4 configurations but reduces Wikipedia from 2 to 1; Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).

\paragraph{Prompt-chain as detection floor.}
All main results use prompt-chain, which produces fewer distributional artifacts than retrain-chain; $\Kempirical{=}2$ therefore establishes a detection floor.
Retrain-chain $\Kempirical{=}7$ (two families; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}) represents the upper bound; real-world mixed contamination falls between these regimes.

\paragraph{Discriminability vs.\ filtering utility.}
The gap between discriminability and filtering utility is itself informative: the modest downstream effect (0.76\,pp MMLU; Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) indicates that depth-level statistical differences do not directly translate to training-signal improvement, contextualizing $\Kempirical$ as a measurement tool rather than a curation prescriptor.

